sn22.59:4.2 | S: Choices are not-self … | T: “Fabrications are not self.…
sn22.59:4.3 | P: Saṅkhārā ca hidaṁ, bhikkhave, attā abhavissaṁsu, nayidaṁ saṅkhārā ābādhāya saṁvatteyyuṁ, labbhetha ca saṅkhāresu: | T: (none)
sn22.59:4.4 | P: ‘evaṁ me saṅkhārā hontu, evaṁ me saṅkhārā mā ahesun’ti. | T: (none)
sn22.59:4.5 | P: Yasmā ca kho, bhikkhave, saṅkhārā anattā, tasmā saṅkhārā ābādhāya saṁvattanti, na ca labbhati saṅkhāresu: | T: (none)
sn22.59:4.6 | P: ‘evaṁ me saṅkhārā hontu, evaṁ me saṅkhārā mā ahesun’ti. | T: (none)
sn22.59:5.1 | S: Consciousness is not-self. | T: “Consciousness is not self.
sn22.59:5.2 | S: For if consciousness were self, it wouldn’t lead to affliction. And you could compel consciousness: | T: If consciousness were the self, this consciousness would not lend itself to dis-ease. It would be possible (to say) with regard to consciousness,
sn22.59:5.3 | S: ‘May my consciousness be like this! May it not be like that!’ | T: ‘Let my consciousness be thus. Let my consciousness not be thus.’
sn22.59:5.4 | S: But because consciousness is not-self, it leads to affliction. And you can’t compel consciousness: | T: But precisely because consciousness is not self, consciousness lends itself to dis-ease. And it is not possible (to say) with regard to consciousness,
sn22.59:5.5 | S: ‘May my consciousness be like this! May it not be like that!’ | T: ‘Let my consciousness be thus. Let my consciousness not be thus.’
sn22.59:6.1 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, monks?
sn22.59:6.2 | S: Is form permanent or impermanent?” | T: Is form constant or inconstant?”
sn22.59:6.3 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”
sn22.59:6.4 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”
sn22.59:6.5 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”
sn22.59:6.6 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn22.59:6.7 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn22.59:6.8 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn22.59:6.9 | S: “Is feeling permanent or impermanent?” … | T: “… Is feeling constant or inconstant?” — “Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.59:6.10 | S: “Is perception permanent or impermanent?” … | T: “… Is perception constant or inconstant?” — “Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.59:6.11 | S: “Are choices permanent or impermanent?” … | T: “… Are fabrications constant or inconstant?” — “Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.59:6.12 | S: “Is consciousness permanent or impermanent?” | T: “What do you think, monks? Is consciousness constant or inconstant?”
sn22.59:6.13 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”
sn22.59:6.14 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”
sn22.59:6.15 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”
sn22.59:6.16 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn22.59:6.17 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn22.59:6.18 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn22.59:7.1 | S: “So you should truly see any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Thus, monks, any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every form is to be seen with right discernment as it has come to be: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.59:8.1 | S: Any kind of feeling at all … | T: “Any feeling whatsoever.…
sn22.59:9.1 | S: Any kind of perception at all … | T: “Any perception whatsoever.…
sn22.59:9.2 | S: Any kind of choices at all … | T: “Any fabrications whatsoever.…
sn22.59:10.1 | S: You should truly see any kind of consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every consciousness is to be seen with right discernment as it has come to be: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.59:11.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness.
sn22.59:11.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn22.59:11.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn22.59:12.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn22.59:12.2 | S: Satisfied, the group of five mendicants approved what the Buddha said. | T: Gratified, the group of five monks delighted in the Blessed One’s words.
sn22.59:12.3 | S: And while this discourse was being spoken, the minds of the group of five mendicants were freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: And while this explanation was being given, the minds of the group of five monks, through lack of clinging/sustenance, were released from effluents.
sn22.59:12.4 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.128
an3.128:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vārāṇasī in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
an3.128:1.2 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Varanasi for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One, having adjusted his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe, went into Vārāṇasī for alms.
an3.128:1.3 | S: While the Buddha was walking for alms near the cow-hitching place at the wavy leaf fig, he saw a disgruntled monk who was looking for pleasure in external things, unmindful, without situational awareness or immersion, with straying mind and undisciplined faculties. | T: As he was walking for alms near the fig-tree at the cattle yoke, he saw a certain monk whose delight was in what is empty, whose delight was in exterior things—his mindfulness muddled, his alertness lacking, his concentration lacking, his mind gone astray, his faculties uncontrolled.
an3.128:1.4 | S: The Buddha said to him, | T: On seeing him, the Blessed One said to him:
an3.128:2.1 | S: “Monk, don’t be bitter. | T: “Monk, monk, don’t let yourself putrefy!
an3.128:2.2 | S: If you’re bitter, festering with putrefaction, flies will, without a doubt, plague and infest you.” | T: On one who lets himself putrefy & stink with the stench of carrion, there’s no way that flies won’t swarm & attack!”
an3.128:2.3 | S: Hearing this advice of the Buddha, that monk was struck with a sense of urgency. | T: Then the monk—admonished with this, the Blessed One’s admonishment—came to his senses.
an3.128:2.4 | S: Then, after the meal, on his return from almsround, the Buddha told the mendicants what had happened. … | T: So the Blessed One, having gone for alms in Vārāṇasī, after the meal, returning from his alms round, addressed the monks [and told them what had happened].
an3.128:3.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhikkhave, pubbaṇhasamayaṁ nivāsetvā pattacīvaramādāya bārāṇasiṁ piṇḍāya pāvisiṁ. | T: (none)
an3.128:3.2 | P: Addasaṁ kho ahaṁ, bhikkhave, goyogapilakkhasmiṁ piṇḍāya caramāno aññataraṁ bhikkhuṁ rittassādaṁ bāhirassādaṁ muṭṭhassatiṁ asampajānaṁ asamāhitaṁ vibbhantacittaṁ pākatindriyaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.128:3.3 | P: Disvā taṁ bhikkhuṁ etadavocaṁ: | T: (none)
an3.128:4.1 | P: ‘Mā kho tvaṁ, bhikkhu, attānaṁ kaṭuviyamakāsi. | T: (none)
an3.128:4.2 | P: Taṁ vata bhikkhu kaṭuviyakataṁ attānaṁ āmagandhena avassutaṁ makkhikā nānupatissanti nānvāssavissantīti, netaṁ ṭhānaṁ vijjatī’ti. | T: (none)
an3.128:4.3 | P: Atha kho, bhikkhave, so bhikkhu mayā iminā ovādena ovadito saṁvegamāpādī”ti. | T: (none)
an3.128:4.4 | S: When he said this, one of the mendicants said to the Buddha: | T: When this was said, a certain monk said to the Blessed One,
an3.128:4.5 | S: “Sir, what is this ‘bitterness’? | T: “What, lord, is putrefaction?
an3.128:4.6 | S: What is ‘putrefaction’? | T: What is the stench of carrion?
an3.128:4.7 | S: And what are the ‘flies’?” | T: What are flies?”
an3.128:5.1 | S: “Desire is bitterness; | T: “Greed, monk, is putrefaction.
an3.128:5.2 | S: ill will is the putrefaction; | T: Ill will is the stench of carrion.
an3.128:5.3 | S: and bad, unskillful thoughts are the flies. | T: Evil, unskillful thoughts are flies.
an3.128:5.4 | S: If you’re bitter, festering with putrefaction, flies will, without a doubt, plague and infest you. | T: On one who lets himself putrefy & stink with the stench of carrion, there’s no way that flies won’t swarm & attack.
an3.128:6.1 | S: When your eyes and ears are unguarded, | T: “On one whose eyes & ears are unguarded,
an3.128:6.2 | S: and you’re not restrained in your sense faculties, | T: whose senses are unrestrained,
an3.128:6.3 | S: flies—those lustful thoughts— | T: flies swarm: resolves dependent on passion.
an3.128:6.4 | S: will plague you. | T: (none)
an3.128:7.1 | S: A mendicant who’s bitter, | T: The monk who is putrid,
an3.128:7.2 | S: festering with putrefaction, | T: who stinks of the stench of carrion,
an3.128:7.3 | S: is far from being extinguished, | T: is far from unbinding.
an3.128:7.4 | S: distress is their lot. | T: His share is vexation.
an3.128:8.1 | S: Whether in village or wilderness, | T: Whether he stays in village or wilderness,
an3.128:8.2 | S: if they don’t find serenity in themselves, | T: having gained for himself no tranquility,
an3.128:8.3 | S: the fool, the simpleton departs, | T: (none)
an3.128:8.4 | S: honored only by flies. | T: he’s surrounded by flies.
an3.128:9.1 | S: But those who have ethics, | T: But those who are consummate in virtue,
an3.128:9.2 | S: lovers of wisdom and peace, | T: who delight in discernment & calm,
an3.128:9.3 | S: they, being peaceful, sleep at ease, | T: pacified, they sleep in ease.
an3.128:9.4 | S: since they’ve got rid of the flies.” | T: No flies settle on them.”
an3.128:9.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

