## thag4.2
thag4.2:1.1 | S: Overwhelmed by drowsiness, | T: I, overcome with torpor,
thag4.2:1.2 | S: I left my dwelling. | T: left my dwelling.
thag4.2:1.3 | S: Stepping up to the path for walking meditation, | T: Stepping up on the walking path,
thag4.2:1.4 | S: I fell to the ground right there. | T: I fell on the ground right there.
thag4.2:2.1 | S: I rubbed my limbs, and again | T: Rubbing my limbs,
thag4.2:2.2 | S: I stepped up on the path for walking meditation. | T: I got up on the path again.
thag4.2:2.3 | S: I walked meditation up and down the path, | T: Walking back & forth on the path,
thag4.2:2.4 | S: serene inside myself. | T: I became well-centered within.
thag4.2:3.1 | S: Then the realization | T: Apt attention arose in me,
thag4.2:3.2 | S: came upon me— | T: (none)
thag4.2:3.3 | S: the danger became clear, | T: the drawbacks appeared,
thag4.2:3.4 | S: and I grew firmly disillusioned. | T: disenchantment stood at an even keel:
thag4.2:4.1 | S: Then my mind was freed— | T: With that, my heart was released.
thag4.2:4.2 | S: behold the clear rightness of the teaching! | T: See the Dhamma’s true rightness!
thag4.2:4.3 | S: I’ve attained the three knowledges, | T: The three knowledges have been attained;
thag4.2:4.4 | S: and fulfilled the Buddha’s instructions. | T: the Buddha’s bidding, done.
thag4.2:4.5 | P: … Bhagutthero …. | T: (none)

## thag4.8
thag4.8:1.1 | S: I am known as “Fortunate Rāhula”, | T: In both ways consummate, I’m known as Rāhula the Fortunate:
thag4.8:1.2 | S: because I’m accomplished in both ways: | T: (none)
thag4.8:1.3 | S: I am the son of the Buddha, | T: because I’m the son of the Buddha,
thag4.8:1.4 | S: whose eye sees clearly in all things. | T: because I’ve the eye that sees Dhammas,
thag4.8:2.1 | S: Since my defilements have ended, | T: because my effluents are ended,
thag4.8:2.2 | S: since there are no more future lives— | T: because I’ve no further becoming.
thag4.8:2.3 | S: I’m perfected, worthy of offerings, | T: I’m deserving of offerings, a worthy one
thag4.8:2.4 | S: master of the three knowledges, <j>seer of freedom from death. | T: a three-knowledge man, one with sight of the deathless.
thag4.8:3.1 | S: Blinded by sensual pleasures, trapped in a net, | T: Those blinded by sensuality covered by the net,
thag4.8:3.2 | S: they are smothered over by craving; | T: veiled by the veil of craving,
thag4.8:3.3 | S: bound by the kinsman of the negligent, | T: bound by the Kinsman of the heedless,
thag4.8:3.4 | S: like a fish caught in a funnel-net trap. | T: are like fish in the mouth of a trap.
thag4.8:4.1 | S: Having thrown off those sensual pleasures, | T: Throwing that sensuality aside,
thag4.8:4.2 | S: having cut Māra’s bond, | T: cutting through Māra’s bond,
thag4.8:4.3 | S: and having plucked out craving, root and all: | T: pulling out craving, root & all,
thag4.8:4.4 | S: I’m cooled, quenched. | T: cooled am I. Unbound.
thag4.8:4.5 | P: … Rāhulo thero …. | T: (none)

## sn44.10
sn44.10:1.1 | S: Then the wanderer Vacchagotta went up to the Buddha and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the wanderer Vacchagotta went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn44.10:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he asked the Blessed One:
sn44.10:1.3 | S: “Worthy Gotama, does the self survive?” | T: “Now then, Master Gotama, is there a self?”
sn44.10:1.4 | S: But when he said this, the Buddha kept silent. | T: When this was said, the Blessed One was silent.
sn44.10:1.5 | S: “Then does the self not survive?” | T: “Then is there no self?”
sn44.10:1.6 | S: But for a second time the Buddha kept silent. | T: A second time, the Blessed One was silent.
sn44.10:1.7 | S: Then the wanderer Vacchagotta rose from his seat and left. | T: Then Vacchagotta the wanderer got up from his seat and left.
sn44.10:2.1 | S: And then, not long after Vacchagotta had left, Venerable Ānanda said to the Buddha: | T: Then, not long after Vacchagotta the wanderer had left, Ven. Ānanda said to the Blessed One,
sn44.10:2.2 | S: “Sir, why didn’t you answer Vacchagotta’s question?” | T: “Why, lord, did the Blessed One not answer when asked a question by Vacchagotta the wanderer?”
sn44.10:2.3 | S: “Ānanda, when Vacchagotta asked me whether the self survives, if I had answered that ‘the self survives’ I would have been siding with the ascetics and brahmins who are eternalists. | T: “Ānanda, if I—being asked by Vacchagotta the wanderer if there is a self—were to answer that there is a self, that would be conforming with those contemplatives & brahmans who are exponents of eternalism [the view that there is an eternal, unchanging soul].
sn44.10:2.4 | S: When Vacchagotta asked me whether the self does not survive, if I had answered that ‘the self does not survive’ I would have been siding with the ascetics and brahmins who are annihilationists. | T: If I—being asked by Vacchagotta the wanderer if there is no self—were to answer that there is no self, that would be conforming with those contemplatives & brahmans who are exponents of annihilationism [the view that death is the annihilation of consciousness].
sn44.10:2.5 | S: When Vacchagotta asked me whether the self survives, if I had answered that ‘the self survives’, would that have facilitated the arising of the knowledge that | T: If I—being asked by Vacchagotta the wanderer if there is a self—were to answer that there is a self, would that be in keeping with the arising of knowledge that all phenomena are not-self?”
sn44.10:2.6 | S: all things are not-self?” | T: (none)
sn44.10:2.7 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn44.10:2.8 | S: “When Vacchagotta asked me whether the self does not survive, if I had answered that ‘the self does not survive’, Vacchagotta—who is already confused—would have got even more confused, thinking: | T: “And if I—being asked by Vacchagotta the wanderer if there is no self—were to answer that there is no self, the bewildered Vacchagotta would become even more bewildered:
sn44.10:2.9 | S: ‘It seems that the self that I once had no longer survives.’” | T: ‘Does the self I used to have now not exist?’”
sn44.10:2.10 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn55.23
sn55.23:1.1 | S: At Kapilavatthu. | T: At Kapilavatthu.
sn55.23:1.2 | S: Then Mahānāma the Sakyan went up to Godhā the Sakyan, and said to her, | T: Then Mahānāma the Sakyan went to Godha the Sakyan and, on arrival, said to him,
sn55.23:1.3 | S: “Godhā, how many things must an individual have for you to recognize them as a stream-enterer, not liable to be reborn in the underworld, assured, destined for awakening?” | T: “Godha, endowed with how many qualities do you understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening?”
sn55.23:2.1 | S: “Mahānāma, an individual must have three things for me to recognize them as a stream-enterer. | T: “Endowed with three qualities, Mahānāma, I understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening.
sn55.23:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
sn55.23:2.3 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Buddha … | T: “There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Awakened One: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
sn55.23:2.4 | P: itipi so bhagavā …pe… satthā devamanussānaṁ buddho bhagavāti. | T: (none)
sn55.23:2.5 | S: the teaching … | T: “He is endowed with verified confidence in the Dhamma: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
sn55.23:2.6 | S: and the Saṅgha … | T: “He is endowed with verified confidence in the Saṅgha:
sn55.23:2.7 | P: suppaṭipanno bhagavato sāvakasaṅgho …pe… anuttaraṁ puññakkhettaṁ lokassāti. | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… who have practiced straight-forwardly… who have practiced methodically… who have practiced masterfully—in other words, the four types of noble disciples when taken as pairs, the eight when taken as individual types—they are the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples: worthy of gifts, worthy of hospitality, worthy of offerings, worthy of respect, the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
sn55.23:2.8 | S: When an individual has these three things I recognize them as a stream-enterer. | T: “Endowed with these three qualities, Mahānāma, I understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening.
sn55.23:3.1 | S: But Mahānāma, how many things must an individual have for <em>you</em> to recognize them as a stream-enterer?” | T: “But what about you, Mahānāma? Endowed with how many qualities do you understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening?”
sn55.23:3.2 | S: “Godhā, an individual must have four things for me to recognize them as a stream-enterer. | T: “Endowed with four qualities, Godha, I understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening.
sn55.23:3.3 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
sn55.23:3.4 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Buddha … | T: “There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Awakened One… verified confidence in the Dhamma… verified confidence in the Saṅgha: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
sn55.23:3.5 | P: itipi so bhagavā …pe… satthā devamanussānaṁ buddho bhagavāti. | T: (none)
sn55.23:3.6 | S: the teaching … | T: (none)
sn55.23:3.7 | S: the Saṅgha … | T: (none)
sn55.23:3.8 | S: And they have the ethical conduct loved by the noble ones … leading to immersion. | T: “He is endowed with virtues that are appealing to the noble ones: untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, leading to concentration.
sn55.23:3.9 | S: When an individual has these four things I recognize them as a stream-enterer.” | T: “Endowed with these four qualities, Godha, I understand an individual to be a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening.”
sn55.23:4.1 | S: “Hold on, Mahānāma, hold on! | T: “Wait, Mahānāma. Wait.
sn55.23:4.2 | S: Only the Buddha would know whether or not they have these things.” | T: The Blessed One is the one who would know whether (the stream-winner) is or isn’t endowed with these qualities.”
sn55.23:4.3 | S: “Come, Godhā, let’s go to the Buddha and inform him about this.” | T: “Then come, Godha. Let’s go to the Blessed One and, on arrival, inform him of this matter.”
sn55.23:4.4 | S: Then Mahānāma and Godhā went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. Mahānāma told the Buddha all that had happened, and then said: | T: So Mahānāma the Sakyan and Godha the Sakyan went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As they were sitting there, Mahānāma the Sakyan said to the Blessed One [and here he recounted the entirety of his conversation with Godha the Sakyan].
sn55.23:5.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhante, yena godhā sakko tenupasaṅkamiṁ; upasaṅkamitvā godhaṁ sakkaṁ etadavocaṁ: | T: (none)
